# What this file is for, in one sentence: this repository is PUBLIC and several
# workflows stage with `git add -A`, so a file that appears for the first time in an
# unattended run is published. Most of the entries below were added after something
# did exactly that. Keep the comments: an entry whose reason is gone is an entry
# nobody dares delete.

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.pyd
.Python
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/
.venv/
venv/
.mypy_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# Environment
.env
*.env
.env.*
# ...but the templates are the documentation, so they stay tracked. Without
# this negation a fresh `.env.example` in a new directory is silently ignored.
!.env.example
!**/.env.example

# Key material and credential-shaped paths. Nothing in this repo should ever hold one;
# the danger is the stray copy next to the deploy scripts that a `git add -A` in a
# workflow nobody is watching sweeps up. On a public repo that is publication, and
# rotating afterwards is the cleanup, not the fix.
*.pem
*.key
id_rsa*
credentials.json
service-account.json
config/secrets.yml

# Logs
*.log

# IDE
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Agent working directories and per-machine settings. `.claude/worktrees/` holds
# checkouts of this repo (a nested worktree is a gitlink `git clean -fd` cannot
# remove, and it arrives inside somebody's `git add -A`); settings.local.json is one
# developer's permissions, not the project's. Only `.claude/skills/**` is tracked.
.claude/worktrees/
.claude/settings.local.json

# Archon per-codebase secrets
.archon/config.yaml

# Workflow run artifacts — $ARTIFACTS_DIR is where agents write plan.md,
# investigation.md, screenshots and raw command output during a run. Same
# exposure path as the config.yaml incident: several workflows do `git add -A`,
# which committed .archon/artifacts/fix-report-pr-67.md by accident.
.archon/artifacts/

# Pi agent runtime artifacts — created by the kimi-coding provider during a run.
# Transient, and they land at the repository root where any `git add -A` finds them.
.pi-loop.json
.pi-loop.json.lock
*.lock.json

# Local benchmark logs + scratch issue bodies (never part of a PR). The benchmark
# itself is archived under docs/archive/benchmark-dynachat/ (D-011); these paths stay
# because a stale log on someone's disk is still a file a stage-everything can pick up.
.benchmark-logs/

# Runtime deploy state — rewritten by deploy.sh on every blue/green flip,
# so keeping it tracked makes every host-side `git pull` abort with a dirty
# working tree. The bootstrap path in deploy.sh writes the initial value
# (`reverse_proxy app-blue:8000`) when the file is absent; the
# upstream.conf.example next to it is the human-readable reference shape.
deploy/upstream.conf

# The stop button's local half. Presence halts the factory; never commit one.
.factory-stop

# Factory run artifacts. Per-run scratch; the durable record is the PR and the issue.
.factory/runs/
.factory/locks-runtime/

# Harness per-run state: the stub port serve.py hands to e2e.py. Never committed.
harness/.run/
